.TH pwd 1 (date) "GNU coreutils (unreleased)"
.SH NAME
pwd \- print name of current/working directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY pwd
.RI [ OPTION \~.\|.\|.\&]
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.\" Add any additional description here
.PP
Print the full filename of the current working directory.
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#pwd-L
\fB\-L, \-\-logical\fP
.UE
use PWD from environment, even if it contains symlinks
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#pwd-P
\fB\-P, \-\-physical\fP
.UE
resolve all symlinks
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/pwd#pwd--help
\fB\-\-help\fP
.UE
display this help and exit
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/pwd#pwd--version
\fB\-\-version\fP
.UE
output version information and exit
.PP
If no option is specified, \fB\-P\fR is assumed.
.PP
Your shell may have its own version of pwd, which usually supersedes
the version described here.  Please refer to your shell's documentation
for details about the options it supports.
.SH AUTHOR
Written by Jim Meyering.
